{
  "dom_id": "step-q94243",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(1,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<2.65).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =1$ and $\\sigma =3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2.65) & =P(Z<\\frac{2.65-1}{3}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.55) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.55) \\\\  & =Φ(0.55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14263116/assets/155088a94114351eb8c2cc110efbf46b1820c7fa2ad5bb2677922f905d0bd286.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.55)=0.7088.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<2.65)=0.7088.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
